welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Linda lorimer the vice president and  secretary of Yale since 1993 miss  Lorimer has served as the senior  counselor to President Levin and is the  leader of major strategic initiatives  for Yale in recent years she has  developed an ambitious strategy and  numerous programs for internationalizing  the university will talk with her today  about those plans welcome is Laura Mars  wonderful to be here so let's begin by  talking about Yale is doing to position  itself as a global university please  give us an overview well actually yell  has been at this for a very long time a  president of Yale about a hundred and  fifty years ago said the university's  role was not to create citizens for the  moment but citizens for the world but  there really has been stepped-up effort  Marilyn in December of 2005 president 11  and I spent time talking with all the  deans of our schools and with over 60  faculty leaders who already are out  around the world with important  scholarly projects to say what should be  the design what should be the shape to  propel Yale to be one of the great  global universities and we created  something called the international  framework which you can find at y le d  you and put an international framework  where we captured the essential three  goals that we heard from our faculty  leaders the first was that we wanted to  be intentional and preparing our own  students here on campus for the lives of  your inner dependency that they will  need to shape the world ahead of us so  that helps influence the curriculum we  have here our second goal was to attract  the best and brightest from around the  world and the third was the audacious to  position Yale as a global University of  consequence so let me just unpack each  of those in terms of preparing our  students and obviously we have an  incredible curriculum here we teach over  50 foreign languages there are over 900  courses that touch upon  tional activities but we said we wanted  to do more so we articulated the goal of  having every Yale College didn't go  abroad once during his or her four years  here and if they were on financial aid  we would give the resources to make it  happen and we have done just that this  past year we had over 1,200 students go  abroad in one year alone but it's not  simply the college that's recraft and  expanding their curriculum I'll just  give two examples from the professional  schools our School of Management  introduced a new curriculum for all  first-year students which has a January  program where every student goes abroad  in groups with a faculty member for a  serious study project so right from the  beginning of their studies we are  capturing this aspect of  internationalization and understanding a  culture in the context of a business or  social responsibility matter for the go  across campus of the architecture school  every third year student in their final  year has an opportunity for a  semester-long studio looking at a  problem in architecture but embedded in  the semester is a travel component also  abroad with their faculty member so  these are enriching activities that we  help will form what a Yale education is  whether you're in the college or whether  you're in one of the graduate or  professional schools but that's our goal  one gold to in attracting the best and  the brightest here obviously the real  lure is the quality of our faculty and  the academic programs but we realize we  should provide a lot more financial aid  through those coming from abroad and we  should give them were support services  so we created a new international center  which held over 800 events last year to  be a home away from home for our growing  number of international students and  scholars a number that has doubled in a  decade we have now about seventeen  percent of our students come from  outside the United States and Canada  some schools are much higher  representation in the music school and  management over thirty percent of the  students come from abroad so that's a  second goal of being a much more  international and welcoming community  the third one the audacious one being a  global University of consequence is  really pursued by our faculty we now  have over 900 faculty members who are  conducting research projects outside the  United States literally in every  continent the some of them are becoming  consequential really quite large I'll  give you two examples in Shanghai we  have a joint fudan university Yale  University Center for genetics its large  75,000 square feet of laboratory space  doing genetics research in sync with the  genetics research being done here at  Yale the director of the shanghai  institute is a full yale professor the  associate director is a shanghai  professor they have virtual lab meetings  by using the internet and showing how  consequential this could be the first  research that warranted being on the  cover of cell magazine leaving a leading  science magazine of course from Asia the  first research from Asia to garner the  cover was emanating from the Sudan Yale  Center in Shanghai so this is an example  of how by working in tandem with an  institution around the world we can make  a real difference but let me go to a  different part of the world if you look  in Ghana we have a joint center with the  University of Ghana and faculty from our  department of infectious medicine  looking at issues of hookworm malaria  and other kinds of infectious diseases  where obviously our faculty benefit as  much as they contribute by being in that  environment which of course has the the  underlying biological and medical issues  right there at had these are both in the  areas of science but we have fascinating  and important work being done in the  humanities and the social sciences and  by all of our professional schools as  well  okay you touched a little bit about on  China so China India and the Middle East  are very much in the news today what are  yells plans in those areas well they  have different strategies for each so  I'll try and describe them briefly okay  in China Yale has a distinction of the  university with the longest ties to that  country when the first young boy left  China to study outside of the mainland  it came all the way to New Haven  Connecticut in 1850 so our heritage is  very very very long and if you look at  the number of projects our faculty  members are conducting in China right  now they number over 80 so they are in  over 17 cities really dotting the  landscape of that great country we have  programs whether it's from the law  school with the China Law Center we have  some of our humanities faculty doing  very important work on families home  village work we also have a program for  university presidents of china's leading  universities who have come for the last  several years to study on our campus so  it's a really very set of programs with  China in India our ties are not as  substantial we have had fewer faculty  working historically in India but that's  changing we announced just in November a  major India initiative we're already 18  million dollars has been raised which in  this economy we think it's really quite  exciting it will eventually add seven  professors to the Yale faculty whose  concentration and expertise will be on  the Indian subcontinent in South Asia  more generally we are looking to have  more internships in the summer for our  students and all the schools to go there  so a major initiative in China we're  very pleased the Prime Minister has  selected Yale to do a program for  aspiring parliamentarians some of the  very best young leaders in the Indian  Parliament and they will be coming back  for another session later this semester  the Middle East the Middle East we have  had some projects that go back over a  century saw the first archaeological  expeditions into the region where by  Yale faculty members they continued we  have a major archaeological expedition  and ongoing project in Egypt for  instance by I give that in terms of  archaeology we have Marsha in horn a new  faculty member who's recently come to  the University who was just in the  United Arab Emirates in the last month  looking at a collaboration there and we  did assist our University here so that's  I you know you know a little bit about  what we're looking at in there our  medical school has some major proposals  for collaborations with universities in  Saudi Arabia that we're pursuing and  we've had sort of long-standing ties  with the crown prince scholarship in  Bahrain where they has sent students  both at the undergraduate level and at  the graduate level we're very eager to  have more young students for all of our  schools complem that area I think like  many universities after nine eleven  we've seen a downturn in the students  coming from the region and one of my  messages whenever i go there and a show  like this is to underscore Yale's  eagerness to welcome students and  scholars from the Middle East as well  we've just created in the past several  years an undergraduate major in Middle  Eastern Studies and we have a new  program bringing distinguished scholars  as visiting professors from the Middle  East so we're really ramping up our  efforts for that important part of the  world okay Yale recently established the  Jackson Institute for Global Affairs  tell us a bit about it and what you  expect to see in both the short term and  long term it is so exciting to have  received a fifty-million-dollar gift lab  from susan and john jackson showing  their belief in investing at such a  massive scale in Yale's  internationalization efforts the focus  will be on teaching and on student  courses in contemporary global affairs I  think we recognize that particularly for  our students we want to buttress  versus maneuverability we have available  to students throughout 